# Test: retired selector values (netlink, nft, tc) are refused with joined fixes; kernel validates.
# Question: does `ze config validate` refuse `interface backend netlink`,
#           `firewall backend nft`, and `traffic control backend tc` in one
#           joined refusal naming each selector, old value, and `set <selector>
#           kernel`, while accepting the same file with `kernel` in each?
#           A unit test cannot prove the real YANG defaults and all three
#           registered backend selectors produce the joined refusal end to end.
# VALIDATES: spec-dataplane-compatibility-guard AC-19 (N-1, F-4 proof) --
#            retired backend names have no alias or fallback and are refused by
#            the generic unknown-backend rule with schema-derived `kernel` fixes.
# PREVENTS: any residual alias accepting `netlink`, `nft`, or `tc` as selector
#           values, or dropping one of the joined refusals.
stdin=retired:terminator=EOF_RETIRED
interface {
	backend netlink;
}
firewall {
	backend nft;
}
traffic {
	control {
		backend tc;
	}
}
EOF_RETIRED

stdin=kernel:terminator=EOF_KERNEL
interface {
	backend kernel;
}
firewall {
	backend kernel;
}
traffic {
	control {
		backend kernel;
	}
}
EOF_KERNEL

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze config validate -:stdin=retired
expect=exit:code=1
expect=stdout:contains=interface backend "netlink"
expect=stdout:contains=set interface backend kernel
expect=stdout:contains=firewall backend "nft"
expect=stdout:contains=set firewall backend kernel
expect=stdout:contains=traffic control backend "tc"
expect=stdout:contains=set traffic control backend kernel

cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze config validate -:stdin=kernel
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=configuration valid
